"# Chapter 13 — Cortex-tracked usage (facts you write, not module events you don't have)

The other dialect's module system emits a stream of events — `module-registered`, `module-loaded`, `module-resolved`, `module-verified`, `module-cycle-detected`, `module-call` — and Cortex records them so you can query your dependency graph. **Motoi has no module system, so it emits none of those events.** There is no import to register, no module body to load, no version to resolve, no cycle to detect. The libraries are simply present.

But Cortex itself is real in Motoi. `cortex/write` and `cortex/recall` are loaded verbs, and the honest, dialect-independent lesson survives: *if your program wants to remember a fact — which libraries it leaned on, what it decided, what it observed — it writes that fact itself.* This chapter teaches Cortex the Motoi way: you are the one who records.

## 13.1 There are no automatic module events

In the other dialect, `(cortex/query :subject :self :predicate 'module-loaded)` returned a bill of materials because each import fired a `module-loaded` event. In Motoi nothing fires that event — there is no import. If you query for it, you get nothing, because nothing ever wrote it:

```scheme
(cortex/query :predicate 'module-loaded)
;; ⇒ ()   ; no module events exist in Motoi
```

That empty result is the honest answer. The module event stream is a feature of the other dialect's loader, and Motoi has no loader to instrument.

## 13.2 You write the fact yourself

The Motoi way to record which libraries a program leaned on is to write that fact with `cortex/write` and read it back with `cortex/recall`:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'used-lib 'string)
(cortex/recall 'used-lib)
;; ⇒ a memory record whose :data is string
```

`cortex/recall` hands back the most recent fact stored under that key, wrapped in a memory record (with an id, a timestamp, a salience, and the `:data` you wrote). This is the deliberate, author-it-yourself version of \"what did I depend on\" — you note the dependency because you chose to.

## 13.3 Recall returns the latest fact

Write a key twice and `cortex/recall` gives you the most recent value. That is how you track a *current* fact that changes over a run:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'current-lib 'list)
(cortex/write 'current-lib 'hash-table)
(cortex/recall 'current-lib)
;; ⇒ a memory record whose :data is hash-table (the latest write)
```

The second write supersedes the first for recall. If you want a *history*, write distinct keys (`'lib-1`, `'lib-2`) or richer values; Cortex keeps what you give it.

## 13.4 A bill of materials, authored by hand

The other dialect's dependency query is, in Motoi, a fact you assemble and store. Put the list of libraries you used into one Cortex fact:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'bill-of-materials '(string list hash-table random))
(cortex/recall 'bill-of-materials)
;; ⇒ a memory record whose :data is (string list hash-table random)
```

There is no hash and no version in this record, because Motoi libraries have neither — they are one always-present standard library, not versioned module artifacts. The honest bill of materials is short: everything depends on the standard library, which is always there.

## 13.5 What Motoi does not track for you

The other dialect offers `cortex/query-diff` (what changed between runs), `cortex/query-unused-imports` (imports with no calls), and sampled `module-call` records. **Motoi binds none of these** — `(cortex/query-diff ...)` yields `error: unbound symbol: cortex/query-diff`, and likewise for `cortex/query-unused-imports`. They have no basis here: there are no imports to be unused, and no per-module load events to diff. If you want a diff, you write two facts and compare them yourself with ordinary list verbs.

## 13.6 Comparing two recorded facts

Here is that comparison, done in plain Motoi. Record a before-list and an after-list, then use `filter` and `member` to find what was added:

```scheme
(define before '(string list))
(define after  '(string list hash-table))
(define added (filter (lambda (x) (not (member x before))) after))
added
;; ⇒ (hash-table)
```

That is the whole of \"what changed\" in Motoi: two lists you chose to keep, and a set difference you compute. No `query-diff` verb, no module load events — just facts and list operations.

## 13.7 What Cortex is for

Cortex is not a module tracker in Motoi; it is a memory. Use it to record what your program decided, noticed, or wants to remember across a run — a design note, a chosen configuration, an observation. The module-audit framing was the other dialect's; the durable idea is *deliberate memory*, and that is fully real here.

## 13.8 Try it

Write a fact with `cortex/write` and read it back with `cortex/recall` (section 13.2). Write the same key twice and confirm recall returns the latest (section 13.3). Then record a before-list and an after-list and compute the added items with `filter`/`member` (section 13.6) — your own diff, no module events required, because Motoi has none.

Chapter 14 covers publishing in the other dialect — putting a module in a registry — and, for Motoi, what \"sharing code\" means when there is no registry and no module. Turn the page.")
